Every indicator file has the columns \code{h3} (lowercase hex string), \code{cell_id},
\code{n}, \code{sp}, \code{shannon}, \code{simpson}, \code{es}; the partition keys live in the path
(read with \code{hive_partitioning = true}). Its rows are sorted by \code{cell_id} in
row groups of \code{row_group_rows} (about 10k), so a reader that knows the cells
under a tile reads only the row groups whose \code{cell_id} statistics overlap
them (a range read) instead of the whole file. Each file is written by its
own \code{COPY} (\code{\link[=obis_h3_write_partitions]{obis_h3_write_partitions()}}), since DuckDB's \code{PARTITION_BY}
does not keep the order. The decade layers are computed from
\code{occ_h3} with the same SQL builder as \code{idx_h3} / \code{idx_h3_eov} (so the math is
identical to the store): \code{n} = records, \code{sp} = distinct species, Shannon,
Simpson and ES(\code{esn}), grouped by cell and \code{decade = floor(date_year / 10) * 10}; records without a year are left out of the decade layers. EOV membership
is the store's \code{eov} table (\code{occ_h3.aphiaid = eov.taxonID}), as in
\code{\link[=obis_eov_bake]{obis_eov_bake()}}. Layers whose source tables are missing (e.g. no
\code{idx_h3_eov} in a store without taxonomy) are skipped with a message.

Partition values are percent-encoded by DuckDB (a space becomes \verb{\%20}), and
DuckDB decodes them on read. A static web server decodes the URL once, so a
browser must request the on-disk name with \verb{\%} itself encoded (\verb{\%2520});
\code{taxon_groups.parquet} carries both \code{path} (on disk) and \code{url_path} (to
fetch), and \code{files.parquet} lists every file with both forms. Each partition
is exactly one \code{data_0.parquet}.

\strong{Parent partitions for the fine resolutions.} A whole res-7 layer is tens
of MB, too much for a browser to load at once, so the resolutions named in
\code{parent_res} are split by parent cell: every row of \verb{all/res=7/} goes to
\verb{all/res=7/p=<h3 of its res-3 parent>/data_0.parquet} (default res 6 by its
res-2 parent, res 7 by its res-3 parent), and a viewer loads only the parent
cells that cover its viewport. The taxon layer uses \code{parent_res_taxon}
(default the res-0 base cell: res-3 parents would make over a million
files across ~5k taxa). A (layer key, res) with at most \code{parent_min_rows}
rows stays one whole file, since splitting it would only add requests. The
decade layers are never split (their default resolutions are 1-5).
\code{files.parquet} has a \code{parent} column (the partition's parent cell, NULL for
a whole file; its resolution is the parent resolution), and each layer in
\code{release.json} lists the \code{parent_res} actually used.

\strong{Place layer (optional).} With \code{place_collections}, each gazetteer
collection gets one file, \verb{place/coll=<slug>/data_0.parquet}, with the
indicators of every place pooled from its res-7 member cells, records
weighted by each cell's share of area inside the place
(\code{\link[=obis_place_indicators]{obis_place_indicators()}}): columns \code{place_id}, \code{layer} (\code{all} | \code{eov}),
\code{key} ('' or the EOV), \code{decade} (NULL = all years, else the decades above),
\code{n} (fractional), \code{sp}, \code{shannon}, \code{simpson}, \code{es}, \code{cells}, \code{area}. These
are true per-place indices of the pooled, area-weighted counts, not means of
hexagon values. The files are listed in \code{files.parquet} (layer \code{place}) and
\code{release.json} (\code{layers$place}, with each collection's membership version);
\code{stats.parquet} does not cover them.

\strong{Exact place layer (optional).} With \code{place_exact}, each polygon collection gets
\verb{place_exact/coll=<slug>/indicators.parquet} and \code{years.parquet}
(\code{\link[=obis_place_indicators_exact]{obis_place_indicators_exact()}}, written by \code{\link[=obis_place_exact_write]{obis_place_exact_write()}}):
the indicators of every place computed from the OBIS occurrences inside the true
polygon, with no H3 approximation, per EOV (\code{all} and the store's \code{eov} table), per
decade and per year, with Berger-Parker and density. They are values only (no
geometry), computed from the occurrence parquet files \code{place_exact_occ} (the local
snapshot the store was built from), which takes far longer than the layers above.
The files are listed in \code{files.parquet} (layer \code{place_exact}) and \code{release.json}
(\code{layers$place_exact}, with each collection's places and record totals);
\code{stats.parquet} does not cover them.

\strong{Year layers.} \code{year/all} and \code{year/eov} are the decade layers per year:
the same SQL (\code{\link[=obis_h3_year_sql]{obis_h3_year_sql()}}) grouped by \code{year = date_year}, one
partition per year present from \code{min(date_year)} to \code{max(date_year)} (or
the \code{years} asked for), at the resolutions \code{res_year} (default 1-5, never
split by parent). \code{release.json} lists the years written (\code{years}).

\strong{The species table} (\verb{species/}, spike 2 of the Ocean Metrics
restructure): one row per (cell, aphiaid) with \code{n} = records, at the
resolutions \code{res_species} (default 3, 4, 5; res 4 rolled up from the res-5
tier), so a browser answers a WoRMS subtree or "which species are in this
cell" from static files. Columns \verb{h3, cell_id, name_key, aphiaid, n}
(\code{\link[=obis_h3_species_sql]{obis_h3_species_sql()}}); one \code{data_0.parquet} per (res, class) written by
\code{\link[=obis_h3_write_partitions]{obis_h3_write_partitions()}}, sorted by \verb{cell_id, aphiaid} in row groups of
100k; a NULL class is \verb{class=_none}. \code{species/species.parquet}
(\code{\link[=obis_h3_species_lookup_sql]{obis_h3_species_lookup_sql()}}) has one row per aphiaid with its names, a
\code{name_key} per distinct name (richness counts names) and its \code{lineage}
(self, then ancestors up the store's \code{taxon} table); a subtree is
\verb{list_contains(lineage, <aphiaid>)} (\code{\link[=obis_h3_species_subtree_sql]{obis_h3_species_subtree_sql()}}).
\code{species/heads.json} lists the taxa that need a pre-aggregated head
(\code{\link[=obis_h3_species_heads]{obis_h3_species_heads()}}: Animalia and every taxon spanning two or more
named classes) and the layer that serves each. Each species row also
carries \code{year_min} and \code{year_max}, the first and last year the aphiaid was
recorded in the cell (within the decade in \verb{species_decade/}), for the Cell
pane. \verb{species_flat/res=<r>.parquet} (default res 1 to 3) is the same table in one
file for every class, sorted by \verb{cell_id, aphiaid} in row groups of
\code{row_group_rows}, so the species of one cell are a range read of one file
(spike 2: 0.53 MB for a res-1 cell) instead of one row group per class file.
The finest of \code{res_species_flat} is written from the store (with \code{h3}); each
coarser one is it rolled up (\code{\link[=obis_h3_write_species_flat]{obis_h3_write_species_flat()}}: \code{n} summed,
\code{year_min} the least, \code{year_max} the greatest, no \code{h3}), so a coarse
hexagon reads its own rows, not its children's. \code{species_flat_split} splits
a resolution by a parent cell, \verb{species_flat/res=3/p=<res-1 parent>.parquet}
by default (no \code{h3}, row groups of \code{split_row_group_rows}), so a cell reads
a file whose footer comes with the first range read. The lookup
\code{species/species.parquet} carries each species' \code{eov}
(\code{\link[=obis_h3_species_eov_sql]{obis_h3_species_eov_sql()}}, the EOV layers' own membership join; NULL for
none) and \code{species/lineage.parquet} holds its \verb{aphiaid, name_key, lineage}
alone (\code{\link[=obis_h3_write_species_lookup]{obis_h3_write_species_lookup()}}), both in row groups of
\code{row_group_rows}.

\strong{The heads} (\code{heads = TRUE}) are written as ordinary taxon layers,
\verb{taxon/rank=<rank>/taxon=<name>/res=<r>/} at \code{res_heads}
(\code{\link[=obis_h3_heads_sql]{obis_h3_heads_sql()}}: the WoRMS subtree from the lineage, species counted by
\code{name_key}, so a head equals \code{\link[=obis_h3_species_subtree_sql]{obis_h3_species_subtree_sql()}} cell for cell),
where \code{rank} is the WoRMS rank lowercased without a botanical alias
("Phylum (Division)" is \code{phylum}). They then appear in \code{stats.parquet},
\code{taxon_groups.parquet} and \code{files.parquet} as any taxon. A head is not
written when a layer already serves it: one whose subtree holds every
aphiaid (and the store has no record without one) is the \code{all} layer; one
whose rank and name are a rank layer of \code{idx_h3_taxon} keeps that layer
(built from the OBIS rank column, not the WoRMS subtree, so it may differ in
a few cells). \code{heads.json} gives each head's \code{served_by} (\code{head}, \code{all},
\code{rank_layer}, or \code{homonym} for a second taxon with the same rank and name)
and its \code{layer}. The per-decade variant
\verb{species_decade/} is written only with \code{species_decades = TRUE}. Rows with
no aphiaid are left out; a store without one (e.g. the demo store built from
\code{occ_SAtlantic}) gets no species layer.

\code{collection.json} is a STAC Collection of the release for the Ocean Metrics
\code{h3} profile (\code{\link[=obis_h3_stac_collection]{obis_h3_stac_collection()}}).

\code{stats.parquet} (sorted by \verb{layer, key}, in row groups of \code{row_group_rows},
so a legend reads only the groups of its layer) has one row per indicator layer and (\code{key}, \code{decade},
\code{year}, \code{res}, \code{variable}) with \code{min}, \code{p02}, \code{p50}, \code{p98}, \code{max} and
\code{n_cells} (cells with a non-NULL value; a variable NULL everywhere in a
partition gets a row with NULL ranges and \code{n_cells} 0), for colour ramps.
\code{key} is the EOV or taxon name ('' for the all-taxa layers), \code{rank} the
taxon rank ('' otherwise), \code{decade} NA outside the decade layers, \code{year} NA
outside the year layers.
}
